A||N||3||jhogans||GM 60° V6 w/HP from 150-300?||||1||
Z||000000||jhogans||01-31-2000||10:53 PM||||Check out this site.  They claim that they can build a GM 60° V6 w/HP from 150-300.  <P>Can this be done?  If so, how?<BR> <A HREF="http://www.kitcar.com/whitehorse/home.html#anchor-engine" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kitcar.com/whitehorse/home.html#anchor-engine</A> <P><BR>This is quoted from their site.<P>"Whitehorse specializes in high-performance GM 60° V6 engine building (as shown above), from 150 to 300hp,   with a high-performance camshaft and other premium components. We'll custom-build your powerplant to your own (horsepower) specifications.<BR>      Your engine from Whitehorse is designed for street and competition use, and meets all federal emissions regulations. Better yet, it's designed to provide you with solid, reliable performance."<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||02-01-2000||01:17 AM||||Sure it can be done... with enough money.  The more horsepower you want, the more it'll cost you...||||reg||3
Z||000002||jhogans||02-01-2000||01:18 AM||||how much we talking and what are your takes on how they accomplish this||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||02-01-2000||04:53 AM||||The horsepower is obtainable, with money and time.  I have approximately 200 streetable hp.  Streetable to me is good power starting under 3000 rpm.  More rpm, more power.  <BR>Aluminum heads, extensive recontouring under the valves, minor radiusing around valves in combustion chamber, three angle valve job, undercut valves, chromoly 1.52:1 roller rockers, and gasket matched to intake runners.<BR>Custom designed headers to work with 9.5:1 compression and the 204/216 crane cam.<BR>Aluminum block with 3.1 crank, pistons fitted to holes with .0010 clearance and 600 grit finish hone, spinning assembly balanced, and fluidampr running the belts.<BR>All mating surfaces inspected and torqued.  Engine broght to operating temperature before initial startup.<BR>block    $3000<BR>heads     $500<BR>crank     $200<BR>rockers   $450<BR>pistons   $400<BR>fluidampr $300<BR>machine   $250<BR>headers   $450<BR>these were costs I had to pay, I scrounged parts and did my own work where I could.  <BR>The aluminum block was necessary because of the oil system, the cast iron blocks feed the cam and crank bearings from the left lifter gallery and the rods get intermittent oil as the crank feed gets covered and uncovered. The aluminum block has an oil gallery just for the bearings and modified main bearing saddles to provide more oil to the rod bearings.<BR>Aluminum heads are not recomended for all out racing, but for high performance street they are workable.<BR>The exhaust was designed by <A HREF="http://www.headersbyed.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.headersbyed.com/</A>  specificly for the car weight, compression ratio, camshaft, and intended usage.<BR>The time part is the $150 per month I paid the dealer for the parts over a three year period, the dealer would not sell performance parts on credit, at least to me he wouldn't.<BR>Anyway thats the time and money breakdown on my engine.  ||||reg||1
